---
// Home page cluster: three interlocking hexagons.
//
// A pointy-top row only nests if alternate hexagons are vertically offset —
// without that they merely touch at flat edges and read as three separate
// shapes. Every second hexagon drops by half its height, and neighbours pull
// in by 25% of their width, which is where the slanted edge begins.
//
// Children arrive through a slot, so they carry no scoped-style attribute.
// The child selectors are :global() to reach them; the .hex-cluster prefix
// keeps them from leaking to the rest of the page.
//
// The offsets use rem, not percentages: a percentage margin resolves against
// the container's width, not the hexagon's, which collapses the cluster.
// Hexagons are w-64 (16rem) above sm, so height is width / 0.866 = 18.48rem.
// Neighbours pull in by a quarter-width (4rem) and the offset hexagon drops by
// half of height (9.24rem) — the same relationship HexGrid's rows use.
//
// The vertical offset uses margin, not transform: Hexagon applies its own
// transform on hover and focus, and a transform here would be overwritten.
---

<div class="hex-cluster">
  <slot />
</div>

<style>
  .hex-cluster {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }

  @media (min-width: 40rem) {
    .hex-cluster {
      flex-direction: row;
      justify-content: center;
      align-items: flex-start;
      gap: 0;
    }

    :global(.hex-cluster > * + *) {
      margin-left: -4rem;
    }

    :global(.hex-cluster > *:nth-child(even)) {
      margin-top: 9.24rem;
    }
  }
</style>
